# React patterns

Idiomatic component design and anti-patterns for React 19-era code,
narrowed to `*.tsx`/`*.jsx` (W1-AC8).

## Derive, don't sync

- If a value can be computed from existing props/state during render,
  compute it during render — do not `useState` + `useEffect` to mirror one
  piece of state into another. A `useEffect` that only calls `setState`
  from a change in props/state is almost always replaceable by a plain
  computed value (optionally memoized with `useMemo` when the computation
  is expensive).
- Reset local state on a prop change by giving the component a `key` tied
  to that prop (React unmounts and remounts a fresh component), not by
  watching the prop in an effect and calling a setter.
- Effects are for synchronizing with something outside React: the DOM,
  a subscription, a network request, browser storage, a third-party widget.
  An effect with no external system on the other end is a sign the logic
  belongs in an event handler or in render itself.

## Lists and keys

- Every element in a list rendered with `.map` gets a stable `key` drawn
  from the data (an id), never the array index, unless the list is
  provably static and never reordered/filtered/inserted into.
- Do not use `key` as a prop for anything other than list identity inside
  the component receiving it — reading `props.key` returns `undefined`.

## Forms and events

- Prefer a controlled input (`value` + `onChange`) for anything the
  component needs to validate, transform, or react to as the user types;
  use an uncontrolled input (`defaultValue` + a ref) only for simple
  fire-and-forget fields where per-keystroke reactivity is not needed.
- For form submission with a pending/error/optimistic result, prefer an
  Action (a function passed to a `<form action={...}>`,
  `useActionState`, and `useOptimistic` for the optimistic update) over
  hand-rolled `isSubmitting`/`error` state plus a submit handler that calls
  `preventDefault` — the Actions API already tracks pending and error state
  and integrates with Suspense.
- Reach for `useTransition` to mark a state update as non-urgent (keeps the
  UI responsive during an expensive re-render) rather than debouncing or
  deferring the update by hand.

## Refs, Suspense, and composition

- In React 19, `ref` is an ordinary prop on function components — no
  `forwardRef` wrapper is needed for a new component that accepts a ref.
  Keep `forwardRef` only in code that must still support pre-19 React.
- Read an async resource inside render with the `use` API (for a promise
  or a context) instead of stuffing the result into state via an effect;
  pair it with a `Suspense` boundary for the pending state and an error
  boundary for the rejected state.
- Prefer composition (children, render props, or a small set of focused
  components) over one component branching on a `type`/`variant` prop into
  unrelated render trees.
- In a framework that supports server and client components, keep data
  fetching and secrets in server components; a component needs
  `"use client"` only once it uses state, effects, refs, or browser-only
  APIs — do not mark a whole subtree client just because one leaf needs it.

## Compiler awareness

- When the project has the React Compiler enabled (check its babel/swc
  config or `babel-plugin-react-compiler`/`react-compiler` dependency),
  do not hand-add `memo`, `useMemo`, or `useCallback` purely to prevent
  re-renders — the compiler already memoizes automatically, and manual
  memoization on compiled code is dead weight that can also mask a bug the
  compiler would otherwise have caught. Without the compiler, manual
  memoization is still a valid, deliberate optimization for a measured
  re-render cost.
